TI  - A Multi-University Small Satellite Systems Design Course: Systems Engineering Approach
T2  - AIAA AVIATION 2019 FORUM
CP  - AIAA Aviation Forum and Exposition
AB  - This paper will review the technical outcomes of a unique two-semester graduate-level Small Satellite Systems Design Course, offered under a collaboration between North Carolina A&T, Old Dominion University, Georgia Tech and Florida Institute of Technology, with additional faculty support from Hampton University and NASA Langley Research Center. The course provided students with skills that are relevant to present day satellite design while fostering an open-ended mission design assignment that is relevant to the support of Earth observation using systems of small satellites. Major outcomes of this course include that the students have the experience of working in multi-disciplinary, multi- university teams, practicing critical thinking skills, and managing the design of complex systems. In addition, the course was developed around utilizing Model-Based Systems Engineering (MBSE) practices and tools throughout the design life-cycle while utilizing a common design language, Systems Modelling Language (SysML). The approach started with a mission idea and proceeded to a feasible concept that included requirements development and management, concept modeling and performance simulation, and prototype building using low-cost additive manufacturing techniques, ending with a "Shark-tank" style presentation.
