AU  - Meghabber, A
TI  - An empirical assessment of ALF-centric model-based flight software development: the Alsat-1B case study
T2  - ADVANCES IN SPACE RESEARCH
AB  - Developing flight software (FSW) for finite-state-machine-driven subsystems on small satellites remains a significant challenge, primarily due to the semantic gap between high-level design models and the final executable code. This paper presents a Model-Based Development (MBD) framework applied to the periodic Attitude and Orbit Control System (AOCS) Process Shell of the Alsat-1B satellite. The proposed workflow adopts the Action Language for Foundational UML (ALF) as a central source of truth, enabling both model-level co-simulation and automated code generation. Unlike preliminary feasibility studies, this work provides a quantitative assessment of framework efficiency and generated-code quality. Post-generation effort is analysed using Git repository mining and a commit-level taxonomy, while generated artefacts are benchmarked against a manually developed reference implementation through a root-cause-decomposed static analysis. Results show that approximately 82% of the deployment code is automatically generated, corresponding to a Refactoring Ratio of 18.14% concentrated in hardware-integration commits. A root-cause decomposition of the comparative static analysis yields a 48% net reduction in reliability issues (from 25 to 13). Of the 25 issues in the manual baseline, 21 (84%) were platform-dependent and 4 (16%) were language-dependent; both categories are absent in the generated implementation, which produced only a single reliability defect, isolating the remaining 12 issues exclusively to the manually written integration layer. The framework's language-independent contribution is evidenced by structural complexity metrics: a 90.3% per-function reduction in Cognitive Complexity and the complete elimination of functions exceeding the NASA-recommended cyclomatic complexity threshold. These results indicate that the proposed approach significantly enhances structural quality and reduces manual effort on core flight software components, while acknowledging that hardware-interfacing layers continue to require manual integration. (c) 2026 COSPAR. Published by Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
